  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The provided evidence from pmid-23171796 explicitly demonstrates that melanoma cells harboring compound mutations in BRAF and NF1 (specifically Braf/Nf1 mutant tumors) are insensitive to the BRAF inhibitor PLX4720 (a Vemurafenib analogue). The text states that while Braf mutant cells were sensitive, cells from Braf/Nf1 mutant tumors were insensitive, with an IC50 > 10uM. Biochemical studies further confirmed that phospho-ERK was not effectively suppressed in Braf/Nf1 mutant cells compared to Braf mutant cells. This directly supports the claim that the molecular profile of BRAF V600E and NF1 Loss is associated with resistance to Vemurafenib in skin melanoma.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-23171796",
        "start": 598,
        "end": 998,
        "quote": "were sensitive to the PLX4032 analogue, PLX4720 (Fig. 4A). However, cells from Braf/Nf1 mutant tumors were insensitive to this agent (Fig. 4A, IC50>10uM). Biochemical studies confirmed that increasing concentrations of PLX4720 effectively suppressed phospho-ERK levels in Braf mutant cells (Fig. 4B). Notably, however, phospho-ERK was not as effectively suppressed in Braf/Nf1 mutant cells (Fig. 4B)."
      }
    ],
